# blob:dfe39e16a2b233918c3a8f0e5a124c5797d1ca30
# Korean AI-Tell Taxonomy v3.1
> v3.1 changes: Selectively ported four preventive rules from snflkd/fluent-korean (output-style, MIT): A-20 (chains/density of 의), A-21 (morpheme-by-morpheme technical-term translation), F-6 (misused metaphors), and F-7 (pedantic archaic/rare words). All are HOLISTIC/S3 naturalness rules without corpus validation. E-2 now distinguishes nominal-ending variation to retain from telegraphic style to remove. J-3 also incorporates the em-dash rule from benelog’s "클로드 문체 없애기 지침" gist: replace by function (parenthetical insertion with parentheses, sentence connection with separate sentences, title/subtitle with body text), exempt code/quotations, and **prohibit colon substitution**.
This taxonomy organizes recurring AI tells in Korean text generated by LLMs (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, etc.) into 11 broad categories and their subpatterns. It is the shared source of truth for detectors, rewriters, and reviewers. Each pattern provides (1) a definition, (2) signature examples, (3) severity (S1 critical / S2 high / S3 low), and (4) a rewriting remedy.
## Rule classes and runtime guarantees (v2.0)
Each subrule declares its guarantee class in `- class:`, parsed by `scripts/prescan.js` and `reconcile.js`.
- **ANCHORED** — Triggered by a specific surface word/phrase, with a `- 표층형:` regex. prescan deterministically extracts **every occurrence as a candidate**, and reconcile **enforces in code** that the detector exhausts every candidate as flag/keep. Coverage does not depend on LLM attention.
- **COMPUTED** — A deterministically calculable document metric, expressed as `- 지표: <name> <op> <number>` (compound conditions can use `and`). Available metrics: `sentence_len_stdev`, `em_dash_count`, `bullet_ratio`, `sentence_count`, `sentence_len_mean`, `char_count`, plus the vendored im-not-ai metrics `comma_inclusion_rate`, `ending_comma_rate`, `comma_segment_length`, `safe_balance_count`, `antithesis_count`, `double_particle_count`, `relative_clause_nesting`. A triggered condition without a corresponding finding makes reconcile FAIL.
- **HOLISTIC** — Context-dependent cases requiring LLM judgment. Rules without `- class:` default to HOLISTIC for backward compatibility. Explicitly label this soft guarantee and minimize its use.
> Expand surface/class fields incrementally on an append-only basis. The detector still judges unannotated rules from their prose, allowing graceful degradation. When promoting a new pattern, `korean-ai-tell-taxonomist` also fills these fields.
## Severity criteria
- **S1 critical**: A single occurrence makes AI authorship seem nearly certain. Always remove.
- **S2 high**: One or two occurrences may be natural; three or more in a document become conspicuous. Remove based on density.
- **S3 low**: Not a problem alone; reinforces an AI impression when overlapping other patterns. Adjust rhythm only.
## Contents
A. Translationese
B. Excessive English quotations and terminology
C. Structural AI patterns (formatting/layout)
D. Signature phrases
E. Uniform rhythm and sentence lengths
F. Excessive modifiers and redundancy
G. Excessive hedging
H. Conjunction overuse
I. Excessive formal and dependent nouns
J. Visual decoration overuse
---
## A. Translationese — S1~S2
Traces of English/Japanese syntax forced into Korean word order and particles: the most decisive signature of AI text.
### A-1. Overuse of "~에 대하여/대해서" [S1]
- Pattern: `X에 대해(서) Y` (English `about/regarding X`)
- Example: "AI 규제**에 대해** 논의할 필요가 있다" → "AI 규제를 논의해야 한다"
- Remedy: Attach the object particle directly, or use the topic particle "는".
### A-2. Overuse of "~를 통하여/통해" [S2]
- Pattern: Rendering nearly all means/routes with "통해" (English `through/via`).
- Example: "데이터 분석**을 통해** 인사이트를 얻는다" → "데이터를 분석해 인사이트를 얻는다"
- Remedy: **Only when repeated within a document** (3+ times in a paragraph), vary with "~로", "~해서", "~함으로써". Preserve 1–2 occurrences.
- Severity history: Downgraded S1 → S2 in v3.0 (upstream v2.3 backport). The Choi Heekyung (2016) corpus found usage in nontranslated Korean (84.4) more than twice that in translations (42.1), rejecting the common translationese assumption. Individual occurrences are not AI markers; only repetition is a style issue. `see: empirical-validation.md#기각`
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `를 통하여|을 통하여|를 통해|을 통해|를 통한|을 통한`
- Keep: Retain 1–2 occurrences (evidence: occurrence count). Flag only 3+ repetitions in a paragraph.
### A-3. "~에 있어(서)" [S1]
- Pattern: Introducing premises/situations (English `in terms of / when it comes to`).
- Example: "이 문제**에 있어서** 중요한 것은" → "이 문제에서 중요한 것은" / "이 문제를 볼 때"
### A-4. "~라는 점에서" [S2]
- Pattern: Giving a rationale (English `in the sense that`).
- Example: "확장성이 뛰어나**다는 점에서** 의미가 있다" → "확장성이 뛰어나서 의미가 있다"
- Caution: Sometimes natural. Remove only when repeated 3+ times in a document.
### A-5. "~와 관련하여" / "~와 관련된" [S2]
- Pattern: Pointing to a topic (English `regarding / related to`).
- Example: "보안**과 관련하여** 주의해야 한다" → "보안에 주의해야 한다"
### A-6. Overuse of "~에 기반하여" / "~을 바탕으로" [S2]
- Pattern: Grounding a claim (English `based on`).
- Example: "데이터**에 기반하여** 판단한다" → "데이터로 판단한다" / "데이터를 보고 판단한다"
### A-7. "가지고 있다" [S1]
- Pattern: Describing possession/properties (English `have/possess`).
- Example: "강한 경쟁력을 **가지고 있다**" → "경쟁력이 강하다"
- Remedy: Recast as an adjective to remove the generic predicate, or simplify to "있다".
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `가지고 있다|가지고 있는|갖고 있다|갖고 있는`
### A-8. Double passive "~되어진다" / "~지게 된다" [S1]
- Example: "판단**되어진다**" → "판단된다" / "판단한다"
- Remedy: Prefer active voice; otherwise use a single passive.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `되어진다|되어졌다|되어지는|지게 된다|지게 됐다`
### A-9. "~에 의해" passives [S2]
- Pattern: by-passive (literal English passive).
- Example: "AI**에 의해** 생성된 이미지" → "AI가 만든 이미지"
- Remedy: Restore the actor as subject.
### A-10. Overuse of "~할 수 있다" [S2]
- Pattern: Possibility statements (English `can/be able to`).
- Example: "효율을 높**일 수 있다**. 비용을 줄**일 수 있다**. 시간을 단축**할 수 있다**."
- Remedy: Shift to definite assertions ("높인다", "줄인다"). Retain only when possibility is the intended meaning.
### A-11. Overuse of "~을 위해" purpose clauses [S2]
- Pattern: `X을 위해 Y한다` (English `in order to`).
- Example: "고객 만족**을 위해** 노력한다" → "고객이 만족하도록 일한다"
### A-12. "만들어지다" / "이루어지다" [S2]
- Pattern: Automatic passive constructions.
- Example: "합의가 **이루어졌다**" → "합의했다" / "합의에 이르렀다"
### A-13. Noun stacking (omitted particles) [S2]
- Pattern: English-style noun phrases stacked without particles.
- Example: "AI 기술 발전 속도 가속화" → "AI 기술의 발전 속도가 빨라지고 있다"
- Remedy: Restore appropriate particles ("의", "가", "를").
### A-14. Linking clauses with the conjunctive adverb "그리고" [S2]
- Pattern: Connecting ordinary sentences with "그리고" as with English `and`.
- Example: "그는 보고했다. **그리고** 자리에 앉았다." → "그는 보고하고 자리에 앉았다."
- Remedy: Compress with connective endings such as "-고", "-며", "-면서".
### A-15. Abstract subject + generic verb [S2] · added in v1.1
- Pattern: Literal `The X shows / provides / brings Y`: an event/phenomenon subject with a generic predicate such as "보여준다·제공한다·가져온다·시사한다".
- Example: "DeepSeek-V4**의 등장은** ~을 **보여줍니다**" / "이 전략**은** 지형**을 흔들고 있습니다**" / "X**는** Y**를 제공합니다**"
- Remedy: Use an actor (person/team/company) as subject, or remove the subject/verb scaffolding and state the point directly, e.g. "DeepSeek는 ~ 원칙을 이렇게 증명했다".
### A-16. Literal English pronouns (그/그녀/그것/그들) [S1] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: One-to-one mapping of English `he/she/it/they → 그/그녀/그것/그들`. Korean maintains cohesion through zero pronouns, repeated noun phrases, and forms of address. NMT/LLM pronoun density is 2–3 times that of nontranslated Korean.
- Example: "존은 피곤했다. **그는** 앉았다. **그는** 한숨을 쉬었다." → "존은 피곤했다. 자리에 앉아 한숨을 쉬었다." / "**그의** 손" → "손"
- Remedy: (a) Treat 50–70% of pronoun occurrences as deletion candidates and restructure, (b) name the person or use a form of address only at speaker/scene changes, (c) vary "they" with "사람들·우리·일부" instead of always "그들".
- **Scope (important)**: **Only for translating/summarizing English source text.** Do not trigger on originally Korean prose: contrastive measurements found humans using `그는/그의` more often (AI 0.0 vs human 1.9/1,000 eojeol). Applying this outside translation contexts damages human writing. `see: empirical-validation.md#재판단`
- class: HOLISTIC
- Source: 김도훈 2009; Cho et al. 2019 (arXiv:1905.11684)
### A-17. (hold) Mechanical '-들' on inanimate/abstract nouns
> **Reason for hold**: Zero positive cases in the upstream v2.0 validation round. Strong academic support (e.g. Jeon Youngchul 2007) lacks decisive corpus evidence. Reserve the ID for revival (patternID stability). Continue quantitative observation only through `metrics_v2.deul_overuse_rate`.
### A-18. Literal relative clauses — long left-branching modifiers (3+ nested adnominal phrases) [S2] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: One-to-one mapping of English postnominal relative clauses into Korean prenominal clauses sharply increases working-memory load before the head noun. NMT/LLMs accumulate left-branching modifiers while trying to retain English SVO order (Park Oksu 2018).
- Example: "그는 **사고를 일으킨 화학물질을 생산한 회사에서 한때 일했던 한 남자를** 만났다." → "그는 한 남자를 만났는데, 그 남자는 사고를 일으킨 화학물질을 만든 회사에서 일했던 사람이었다."
- Remedy: Split adnominal clauses of 3+ eojeol into sentences or a following appositive clause ("~를 만났는데, 그 X는 ~"). Always restructure sentences with 3+ nested adnominal phrases.
- class: COMPUTED
- 지표: relative_clause_nesting >= 2
- Source: 박옥수 2018; 김채은 2021
### A-19. Double particles (-에서의·-으로의·-에의·-으로부터의) [S2] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: Doubled case particles influenced by Japanese 'の' and English prepositional phrases. Korean normally expands these into clauses/phrases. **Explicitly exclude simple '~의'** (no academic consensus; attested since the 15th century).
- Example: "긴장**으로부터의** 해방" → "긴장에서 벗어남" / "설문지**에의** 응답" → "설문 답변" / "2층**에서의** 살림" → "2층에서 시작한 살림"
- Remedy: Review only double combinations systematically. Reinterpret at sentence level instead of mapping prepositional phrases one-to-one. Expand consecutive "의 의 의" into clauses/phrases.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `에서의|에로의|으로의|에의|으로부터의|로부터의`
- Source: 김정우 2007; 김순영 2012
### A-20. Chains / excessive density of '의' [S3] · fluent-korean port (readability)
- Pattern: Unnecessary repetition of the adnominal particle '의': chains of 3+ such as "A의 B의 C의 D", or excessive sentence/paragraph density. Often caused by one-to-one transfer of English of or Japanese の.
- Example: "사용자**의** 요청**의** 처리**의** 결과" → "사용자 요청을 처리한 결과" / "분석**의** 흐름" → "분석 흐름" / "작업**의** 상황" → "작업이 진행되는 상황"
- Remedy: (a) Expand '의' chains into clauses/phrases or compress into compound nouns, (b) remove meaningless '의' and join nouns directly, (c) restore omitted predicates while removing '의'.
- **Boundary (important)**: **A single 의 is not itself an AI tell** (see A-19: attested since the 15th century; minimal use is natural). Target only **chains/excessive density**. Mechanical removal can create noun stacking (A-13). S3: edit only when combined with other patterns.
- class: HOLISTIC
- Source: snflkd/fluent-korean (output-style sentence-level-1 / phrase-level, MIT)
---
## B. Excessive English quotations and terminology — S2
### B-1. Parenthesized English equivalents [S2]
- Pattern: Adding English alongside every technical term on first use.
- Example: "인공지능**(AI)**은 거대언어모델**(LLM)**과 다르다."
- Remedy: For specialist readers, give the equivalent once and then use Korean only. For general readers, minimize English equivalents altogether.
### B-2. Unnecessary English decoration / one-off jargon [S2] · upstream redefinition ported in v3.0
> **Term preservation**: Category B does not mean deleting every English word. Established technical terms in development/AI writing (`API`, `SDK`, `CLI`, `prompt`, `token`, `embedding`, `agent`, `plugin`, `pipeline`, `framework`, etc.) must retain their meaning. Do not mechanically translate `prompt` as "지시문" or `agent` as "대리인".
> - Preservation depends on audience and context. For general readers, Korean may be more appropriate: `span` → "구간", `metric` → "지표". Ask whether the audience reads the original term more naturally than its translation.
> - B targets unexplained promotional buzzwords, repeated parenthetical equivalents, and showy English unsuited to the audience.
- Pattern: Unexplained buzzwords disrupt Korean sentence rhythm, or showy English recurs.
- Example: "사용자에게 **seamless**하고 **robust**한 경험을 제공한다" → "사용자가 끊김 없이 안정적으로 쓸 수 있게 한다"
- Remedy: (a) Preserve standard technical terms, (b) explain only promotional buzzwords (seamless/robust/leverage) in Korean, (c) explain on first use and then use one consistent form.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `(?i)\b(seamless|robust|leverage|synergy|holistic|game.?changer)`
- Keep: Retain standard technical terms and proper names. Flag only buzzwords.
### B-3. Excessive English quotations [S2]
- Pattern: Inserting full English quotations alongside their translations.
- Remedy: Paraphrase in Korean and give the source unless the original wording is essential.
### B-4. "~라고 알려진", "~로 일컬어지는" [S3]
- Pattern: Literal English `known as / so-called`.
- Example: "**'AGI'라고 알려진** 범용 인공지능" → "범용 인공지능(AGI)"
---
## C. Structural AI patterns (formatting/layout) — S1~S2
### C-1. Mechanical parallel enumeration [S2]
- Pattern: "첫째, ~. 둘째, ~. 셋째, ~." dominates the whole paragraph.
- Remedy: **Preserve by default.** Enumeration is also natural Korean rhetoric; unconditional expansion damages writing. Only when 4+ items in a paragraph sound mechanical, integrate 1–2 into prose or vary with "우선 / 다음으로 / 마지막으로".
- Severity history: Downgraded S1 → S2 in v3.0 (upstream v2.0.1 backport). S1’s unconditional-removal rule caused overediting that dismantled even three-item lists, as reported by upstream web-app users.
### C-2. Excessive bullet lists [S2]
- Pattern: Three or more consecutive bullet blocks in essays, columns, or reports.
- Remedy: Integrate bullets into prose. Retain only lists whose enumeration serves a purpose.
### C-3. Repetitive section headings [S2]
- Pattern: Schematic divisions such as `## 도입 ## 본론 ## 결론`.
- Remedy: Remove headings for prose. For reports, make headings specific, e.g. "AI 규제의 세 가지 균열점".
### C-4. Paragraph-opening summary formula [S2]
- Pattern: Every paragraph opens with a summary/topic sentence, following an English composition textbook.
- Remedy: Vary the order so some paragraphs open with examples, scenes, or quotations.
### C-5. Emoji overuse [S1]
- Pattern: Emoji such as `✅ 🚀 💡 ⚠️ 📊` appear in list markers, headings, or emphasis.
- Remedy: Remove all in essays/reports. Outside social media or product copy, they are a very strong AI tell.
### C-6. One-line summary boxes below headings [S2]
- Pattern: Every section heading is immediately followed by guidance such as "이 섹션에서는 ~를 다룬다".
- Remedy: Delete. Starting directly with the body is more natural in Korean.
### C-7. Mechanical three-part paragraph sequence "먼저·반면·결국" [S2] · added in v1.1
- Pattern: Paragraph openings follow a fixed sequence "먼저 ~ / 반면 ~ / 결국 ~" or "첫째 ~ / 둘째 ~ / 마지막으로 ~". Korean writers occasionally use this, but 3+ consecutive instances are characteristic of AI.
- Example: This document’s v1 draft (paragraph 2 "먼저", paragraph 3 "반면", paragraph 6 "결국").
- Remedy: Delete two of three. Convey sequence through paragraph flow and include paragraphs without opening conjunctions.
### C-8. Repeated symmetrical antithesis "A인가, B인가" [S1] · added in v1.1 · strongest measured signal in v3.0
- Pattern: Binary oppositions repeat in parallel phrases three or more times in one document: literal transfer of English rhetoric.
- **Measured discrimination (upstream v2.3 contrastive corpus)**: Negative antithesis `A가 아니라 B` density: AI 5.8 vs human 0.6 (**9.2×**, G²=41.7, p<0.0001), 18× relative to personal blogs. Shared across Fable, GPT, and Haiku, indicating a model-family-independent AI signature. Strongest measured signal across A–J. `see: empirical-validation.md#확증`
- Example: "독점**인가**, 확산**인가**" / "전략가에게는 ~, 입안자에게는 ~"
- **Negative–positive antithesis variant (A가 아니라 B)**: Chains such as "단순히 A가 아니라 B다", "A라기보다 B다", "쓰느냐가 아니라 지키느냐다". A strong signature of modern LLM prose, especially GPT; the decisive feature is an entire paragraph uniformly following this template.
- Remedy: **Keep only one per chain** and turn the rest into direct assertions ("A가 아니라 B" → "B가 핵심이다"). Rearrange two of three symmetrical constructions asymmetrically.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `(?:가|이) 아니라|라기보다|이기 이전에|인가,`
- Keep: **Do not eliminate all**. Removing every antithesis when the original has 5+ makes verify_gates P2 FAIL. Keep one per chain (evidence: preservation choice).
### C-9. Parenthesized numeric indexing "1) 2) 3)" [S2] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: `1) ... 2) ... 3) ...` lists within a paragraph or adjacent sentences. A notation signature separate from C-1 (첫째·둘째) and C-2 (bullets). LLMs use it almost automatically for three items.
- Remedy: Integrate one of three items into prose and vary the remainder with "우선~", "다음으로~". Retain only when enumeration matters, at most once per document.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `(?m)(?:^|\s)[1-3]\)\s`
- Keep: Preserve original numbering in quotations from laws/contracts.
### C-10. Colon-subtitle heading formula "X: Y" [S2] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: Automatically assigning headings a "main label: subtitle" formula. Separate from C-3’s schematic divisions; this targets the colon/subtitle structure itself.
- Example: "### 서론: 제조업의 미래, AI에 달려있다" / "## 핀테크, '일상'을 넘어 '생태계'로"
- Remedy: Remove the colon/subtitle and compress to one noun or verb phrase. Integrate a necessary subtitle into the first body sentence. At most once per document.
- **Exception**: Preserve actual academic/report section headings (numbered sections and chapter titles); do not delete, rewrite, or absorb them into body text.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `(?m)^#{1,4}\s+[^:\n]{2,30}:\s+\S`
- Keep: Preserve academic/report section headings (evidence: genre).
- 재감사: The surface regex is anchored to the line start, so quoted examples inside this section cannot match by construction. It fires only on actual input heading lines; exempt comparison against this section’s body.
### C-11. Comma after connective ending [S1] · upstream port in v3.0 (KatFish 4.84×)
- Pattern: A comma immediately follows connective endings (-고/-며/-지만/-면서/-아서·어서/-는데). Humans use the ending itself as a breath unit; AI imports English comma-after-conjunction habits. **Strongest single-metric separation**: KatFish essays, human 4.10% vs AI 19.83%.
- Example: "발전하**지만,** 대응은 더디다" / "정제하**고,** 학습시킨다"
- Remedy: Remove commas after connective endings systematically. If the breath unit is long, split at a period or move the comma to the main-clause boundary. 6+ occurrences = S1; 3–5 = S2.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `[가-힣](?:고|며|지만|면서|는데|아서|어서),`
- Keep: Retain grammatically necessary commas before following parentheticals or quotations.
### C-12. Comma inclusion rate (document level) [S2] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: More than 50% of sentences contain at least one comma. Unlike position-specific C-11, this measures the document-wide distribution (KatFish: human 26.31% vs AI 61.03%).
- Remedy: When over the threshold, revise some sentences by (a) splitting at periods, (b) absorbing commas into connective endings, or (c) deleting commas.
- class: COMPUTED
- 지표: comma_inclusion_rate > 0.5 and sentence_count >= 5
---
## D. Signature phrases — S1
Stock phrases rarely used by human Korean writers but repeatedly generated by LLMs. Replace immediately upon detection.
**Do not insert these during rewriting** (upstream port in v3.0): This category’s vocabulary is **a removal target, not generation material**. Introducing D-family clichés absent from the original ("괄목할 만한", "~로 평가된다", "주목받았다", "의미가 크다") is contradictory and prohibited. Replacing vivid original expressions with clichés reverses the rewriting goal.
### D-1. Closing / summary phrases
- "결론적으로", "요약하면", "종합하면", "정리하자면"
- "~라고 할 수 있다" / "~라고 볼 수 있다"
- "~라 하겠다", "~라 할 것이다"
- "~에 다름 아니다"
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `결론적으로|요약하면|종합하면|정리하자면|라고 할 수 있다|라고 볼 수 있다|에 다름 아니다`
### D-2. Exaggerated significance / importance
- "매우 중요하다", "반드시 기억해야 한다"
- "시사하는 바가 크다", "주목할 만하다"
- "간과할 수 없다", "무시할 수 없다"
- "~의 지평을 연다", "~에 방점을 찍는다"
- "그 의미가 적지 않다", "의미심장하다"
### D-3. Enumeration introductions
- "크게 세 가지로 나눌 수 있다"
- "다음과 같은 특징을 가진다"
- "다음과 같이 요약할 수 있다"
### D-4. AI-specific phrases
- "혁신적인", "획기적인", "전례 없는" (hype vocabulary)
- "~의 가능성을 열어준다"
- "~의 새로운 장을 열다"
- "~시대가 도래했다"
### D-5. Personified abstract subjects [S2] · added in v1.1
- Pattern: Describing events, technologies, or concepts as if performing human actions: AI’s default attempt to give prose weight.
- Example: "**두 지능의 충돌**이 질문을 **던집니다**" / "**AI 대전**이 **끝나지 않습니다**" / "**지능의 가성비**가 **증명합니다**"
- Remedy: Replace with a real actor ("두 회사의 경쟁은", "엔지니어들은"), or weaken the personifying verb ("던집니다" → "남습니다" / "생깁니다"). Allow about one symbolic title/summary occurrence.
### D-6. Formulaic conclusion "~할 때입니다 / 시점입니다" [S2] · added in v1.1
- Pattern: A column/report ends with "~해야 할 때입니다", "~로 나아갈 시점입니다", or "~할 순간입니다".
- Example: "에이전트 정부의 시대로 **나아가야 할 때입니다**"
- Remedy: State the same meaning with a concrete verb. A less exaggerated form such as "에이전트 정부 단계로 **넘어갈 때입니다**" is acceptable. Once per document only.
### D-7. Transformation formula "X에서 Y로 / X을 넘어 Y로" [S2] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: Nearly automatic wording for paradigm shifts/evolution. A concluding/slogan formula separate from D-1/D-6, emphasizing **direction of transformation**. Predominantly a Gemini signature.
- Example: "**'규모의 경쟁'에서 '전략의 경쟁'으로**" / "**'데이터 조회'를 넘어 '맞춤형 금융 비서'로**"
- Remedy: Use a direct assertion ("'지식 전달자'에서 '학습 조력자'로" → "교사는 더 이상 지식 전달자가 아니다. 학생 곁에서 학습을 돕는다"). At most once per document.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `(?:을|를) 넘어 |['"'"].{1,20}['"'"]에서 ['"'"].{1,20}['"'"](?:으로|로)`
- Keep: Preserve physical movement or boundary crossing ("고개를 넘어").
**Remedy:** Mostly **delete**. When the meaning is needed, use concrete nouns/verbs ("중요하다" → "핵심이다", or give specific evidence).
---
## E. Uniform rhythm and sentence lengths — S2
### E-1. Low sentence-length standard deviation — especially missing long sentences · empirically redefined in v3.0
- All sentences cluster around 30–50 characters. **The underlying issue is missing long sentences more than uniformity**: contrastive measurements of 100+ character sentences found AI 8.1 vs human 91.3 per 1,000 sentences (11×). AI produces short sentences without long breath units.
- Remedy: Mix 1–2 short sentences (10–15 characters) with **one long sentence (80–100+ characters)** per paragraph. When joining adjacent sentences, **do not add content absent from the original**; only connect them.
- class: COMPUTED
- 지표: sentence_len_stdev < 8 and sentence_count >= 5
### E-2. Repeated identical sentence endings
- "~이다. ~이다. ~이다."
- "~한다. ~한다. ~한다."
- Remedy: Mix "~다", "~았다", "~인 것", and nominal endings. Human writers vary these unconsciously.
- **Conditional distinction for nominal endings · fluent-korean port**: The nominal endings recommended here are **occasional rhythm variations** that break monotonous "~다" repetition. By contrast, **telegraphic nominal endings** that systematically omit predicates/endings and leave most sentences as noun phrases are machine-Korean signals to remove. Handle these through A-13 (noun stacking) and I-1 (것이다 endings), restoring predicates/endings. Occasional nominal endings are variation to keep; dominant nominal endings are machine style to remove. `see: fluent-korean output-style 문장단위-2`
- **List/itemized-style exemption (restored from fluent-korean)**: E-2 is not mandatory for headers or lists. This restores a parenthetical limitation from upstream sentence-level rule 2 that was lost during absorption. Exempt header rows, list items, itemized body rows beginning with `□ ○ - ⇒`, table cells, and captions. The entire itemized body qualifies because all its content is list items.
- 적용_서법: 서술-합쇼체 (`see: references/register-axis.md`)
### E-3. Three-to-four-sentence paragraph formula
- Paragraph lengths are uniform too.
- Remedy: Deliberately mix one-sentence and six-sentence paragraphs.
### E-4. Only simple sentences (no complex/compound sentences) [S2] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: Most sentences are simple, with few complex constructions using connective endings, adnominal clauses, or quotation clauses. Paired with E-1: E-1 measures length; E-4 targets **structural monotony**.
- Example: "AI는 빠르게 발전한다. 기업은 따라가야 한다. 시간이 없다. 데이터가 핵심이다."
- Remedy: Combine 2–3 adjacent simple sentences with connective endings (-며·-고·-는데) or adnominal clauses. Aim for about 60% simple sentences and 30%+ complex/compound sentences. Use simple sentences only for emphasis/transitions.
- class: HOLISTIC
### E-5. Mean comma-segment length (long clause structure) [S2] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: Long mean eojeol counts in comma-separated clauses, reflecting English long-sentence structure (KatFish: human 4.35 vs AI 8.56 eojeol). The opposite extreme from E-4; both are AI signatures.
- Remedy: Split clauses of 8+ eojeol at periods or compress into Korean adnominal clauses. Increase weight when E-4 also triggers.
- class: COMPUTED
- 지표: comma_segment_length > 7 and sentence_count >= 5
### E-6. High part-of-speech diversity around commas [S2] · upstream port in v3.0 · genre guard
- Pattern: Indiscriminate commas across diverse part-of-speech boundaries (KatFish: human 24.38 vs AI 59.39). **Only essays, news, blogs, and reports**; discrimination is weak for poetry/fiction.
- Remedy: Limit commas to (a) main-clause boundaries and (b) clear appositives/parentheticals.
- class: HOLISTIC
### E-7. Inconsistent addressee honorifics (mixed 해라/해요/합쇼체) [S2] · upstream port in v3.0 · genre guard
- Pattern: Formality levels (해라·하게·하오·해요·합쇼) mix within a document/conversation. Unlike E-2’s repeated single endings, E-7 targets **formality consistency**.
- Example: "도와주시겠습니까?(합쇼)" immediately followed by "도와줘?(해라)", or a paragraph ending "~합니다" followed by one ending "~한다".
- Remedy: Choose the addressee level at the start and keep it consistent. **Apply only to dialogue/spoken-language text**, not genres using a single formal register.
- class: HOLISTIC
- Source: 김혜영 2019 (estimated threshold)
---
## F. Excessive modifiers and redundancy — S2
### F-1. Intensifier addiction
- "매우", "정말", "진짜로", "대단히", "극히"
- Remedy: Delete most. Replace necessary emphasis with concrete figures/examples.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `매우|대단히|극히|진짜로`
### F-2. Synonymous double modifiers
- "중요하고 핵심적인 역할"
- "새롭고 혁신적인 접근"
- "지속적이고 꾸준한 노력"
- Remedy: Keep only one of the two modifiers.
### F-3. Function + role compound phrases
- "~로서의 역할과 기능"
- "~의 의미와 가치"
- Remedy: Keep only one.
### F-4. Excessive prefixes/suffixes
- "~적 측면", "~적 관점"
- Overuse of "~성(性)", "~화(化)".
- Example: "**근본적 관점**에서 **구조적 변화**가 **필연적**이다" → "구조가 근본부터 바뀐다"
### F-5. Chains of abstract "~적 N" compounds [S2] · added in v1.1
- Pattern: "~적 N" before a noun repeats 3+ times in a document. Unlike F-4’s abstract modifiers ("적 측면/관점"), this is a chain of **~적 N before concrete nouns**. Frequent when AI imitates the original’s intellectual authority.
- Example: "에이전트**적** 자율성", "기술**적** 안정성", "경제**적** 자립", "기술**적** 토대", "시스템**적** 접근", "구조**적** 변화"
- Remedy: Expand "~적 N" into (a) "~로서의 N" ("에이전트로서의 자율성"), (b) a verb phrase ("기술이 얼마나 안정적인가"), or (c) a concrete noun ("토대"). Reduce document-wide "~적 N" density to at most half.
### A-21. Morpheme-by-morpheme technical-term translation (calques without established equivalents) [S2] · fluent-korean port (vocabulary guidance)
- Pattern: Translating English technical terms morpheme by morpheme creates words that do not convey their meaning in Korean. Literal translation kills the original metaphor and leaves **only an unrelated everyday sense**. Without an established translation, retain the original or explain its meaning.
- Example: A/B experiment `arm` → "**팔**" (only the body-part meaning survives in Korean; use "비교군", "조건", or "arm") / `branch` → "**가지**" (use "브랜치") / `tree shaking` → "**나무 흔들기**" (use "트리 셰이킹") / `sink` → "**싱크대**" (use "sink" or "종점").
- Counterexamples (must not trigger): Established translations/transliterations such as "포인터", "상속", "지연 시간", "캐시", "스레드", "가상 머신". Determine established status by actual usage in Korean literature in that field.
- Remedy: (a) Use an established translation if available, (b) otherwise **retain the original term**, (c) if it obstructs reading, explain the meaning with the original in parentheses.
- **Scope**: Trigger only in technical-term positions. F-6 handles metaphors in ordinary vocabulary; this rule targets **terms with a separate original-language form**.
- class: HOLISTIC
- Source: snflkd/fluent-korean (output-style vocabulary guidance, MIT), scope item 3: prefer an established translation/transliteration; otherwise retain the original term.
### F-6. Misused metaphors (figurative wording in ordinary-word positions) [S3] · fluent-korean port
- Pattern: Figurative/rhetorical words replace sufficient ordinary nouns/verbs, obscuring meaning. Especially harms readability and meaning in technical/explanatory contexts.
- Example: "코드로 **박는** 자리" → "코드에 명시하는 부분" / "요청을 **받습니다**" → "요청을 확인했습니다" / "지형을 **흔든다**" → "시장 구도를 바꾼다"
- Remedy: Retain metaphors only when (a) common in everyday written language and (b) established idioms in the field. Otherwise restore ordinary wording. Do not apply indiscriminately: forced expansion of conventional metaphors can sound unnatural.
- **Boundary**: Exempt literature/essays, where metaphor is a legitimate expressive resource. Apply only to technical documents, reports, and explanatory text.
- class: HOLISTIC
- Source: snflkd/fluent-korean (output-style phrase-level-3, MIT)
### F-7. Pedantic archaic/rare words [S3] · fluent-korean port (naturalness)
- Pattern: Choosing old-fashioned or rare Sino-Korean/archaic words where common words suffice makes prose unnaturally pedantic. fluent-korean identifies coding agents’ overuse of rare vocabulary under brevity pressure.
- Remedy: Use common words most readers understand immediately, while retaining established specialist terminology.
- **Empirical caution**: Classic ChatGPT tells tend toward stock/common vocabulary. Archaic/rare wording is therefore a **preventive naturalness/readability rule**, not a corpus-validated AI signature. S3: defer to reviewer judgment rather than force removal. Do not promote to ANCHORED (false-positive risk).
- class: HOLISTIC
- Source: snflkd/fluent-korean (output-style vocabulary guidance, MIT)
---
## G. Excessive hedging — S2
### G-1. Speculative/observational endings
- "~할 수 있을 것으로 보인다"
- "~인 것으로 판단된다"
- "~라고 여겨진다"
- "~인 듯하다" attached to every sentence ending.
- Remedy: Assert where support permits; hedge only genuine uncertainty.
### G-2. Double/triple hedging
- "~할 가능성이 있을 수 있다"
- "~로 보여질 수 있다"
- Remedy: Keep only one hedge.
### G-3. Safe-balance lexicon (Safe Balance Score) [S2] · upstream port in v3.0
- Pattern: Excessive balance/two-sidedness vocabulary such as "양쪽 모두 / 두 가지 모두 / 장점도 있지만 / 신중하게 / 균형" (KatFish lexicon metric). Unlike G-1’s endings, this operates at **lexicon level**, reflecting risk avoidance and presenting both sides.
- Example: "**양쪽 모두** 일리가 있다 / **장점도 있지만** 단점도 있다 / **신중하게** 접근해야 한다 / **균형** 잡힌 시각"
- Remedy: (a) Assert one side, (b) compare concrete examples, or (c) state conditions ("X일 때는 A, Y일 때는 B"). S2 at 4+ occurrences per document.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `양쪽 모두|두 가지 모두|장점도 있지만|신중하게|균형 잡힌|균형을`
- Keep: Retain 1–3 occurrences and actual financial/physical balance references.
---
## H. Conjunction overuse — S2
### H-1. Excessive opening conjunctions
- Every sentence/paragraph opens with "또한", "따라서", "즉", "나아가", "아울러", "게다가", "더욱이".
- Remedy: Remove at least 70%. Sentence content should convey the logic. Retain only when contrast/causality needs explicit emphasis.
### H-2. Overuse of mixed "하지만" / "그러나"
- A contrastive conjunction appears in every paragraph.
- Remedy: Delete at least half. Obvious contrasts work without conjunctions.
### H-3. Repeated demonstrative "이는 ~"
- "이는 ~을 의미한다"
- Remedy: Join with the previous sentence or replace with a concrete statement.
### H-4. Overuse of restating conjunction "즉" [S2] · added in v1.1
- Pattern: Literal English `i.e.` / `that is`; "즉" precedes every supplementary explanation.
- Example: "AI 민주화, **즉** 경제성 측면에서"
- Remedy: Vary with "곧", "말하자면", "다시 말해", "바꿔 말하면", or omit and connect with commas alone. Limit "즉" to two occurrences per document.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `즉[\s,]`
---
## I. Excessive formal and dependent nouns — S2
### I-1. Overuse of "것이다" endings · empirical recalibration in v3.0
- "~한 것이다", "~일 것이다" are the paragraph’s dominant endings.
- Remedy: **Only with 3+ consecutive repetitions**, replace some with definite "~다". **Preserve by default.**
- Evidence: Contrastive measurements found `~것이다` endings AI 20.4 vs human 43.0 per 1,000 sentences (**humans twice as often**). It is an ordinary Korean expository ending, not an AI tell. Only concentrated repetition is a style issue. `see: empirical-validation.md#기각`
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `것이다|것입니다`
- Keep: Retain fewer than three consecutive occurrences (evidence: consecutive count). Do not flatten unconditionally.
### I-2. Repeated "점", "바", "수", "데"
- "주목할 **점**은", "나아갈 **바**는", "할 **수**가 있다", "하는 **데**에"
- Remedy: Replace with concrete nouns/verbs or delete.
### I-3. "~라는 것"
- "변화가 크**다는 것이다**."
- Remedy: "변화가 크다." (attach the sentence ending directly).
### I-4. "~할 필요가 있다"
- Literal English `should/need to`.
- Remedy: "~해야 한다" or a concrete recommendation.
### I-5. "~이/가 필요하다"
- "혁신이 필요하다", "변화가 필요하다"
- Remedy: Specify who should do what with a subject and verb.
### I-6. Chains of abstract "~능력" nouns [S2] · added in v1.1
- Pattern: "N 능력" repeats 3+ times in a document, describing abilities as noun phrases instead of verbs, echoing literal English `ability to X / X capability`.
- Example: "사고 **능력**", "워크플로우 수행 **능력**", "장기 문맥 유지 **능력**", "추론 **능력**"
- Remedy: Expand into verbs. "사고 능력은 뛰어나다" → "잘 사고한다" / "사고의 수준이 높다"; "워크플로우 수행 능력" → "워크플로우를 얼마나 잘 처리하는지". Limit "~능력" to two occurrences per document.
---
## J. Visual decoration overuse — S2~S3
### J-1. Excessive **bold**
- Key words are bold in every sentence.
- Remedy: Remove nearly all body bold; it creates visual noise.
### J-2. Excessive quotation marks
- Overuse of quotation marks around concepts/emphasis.
- Remedy: Restrict to actual quotations and special usages.
### J-3. Em-dash (—) overuse · benelog dash rule incorporated in v3.1
- Pattern: English-style em dashes introduce additional explanations, sentence connections, term glosses, and title subtitles. Do not use dashes inside body sentences.
- Example: "AI는 도구 — 그 이상도 이하도 아닌 — 이다"
- **Replacement recipes by function**:
- Parenthetical insertion → **parentheses**: "관계형 스키마 — 외래 키, JOIN — 를" → "관계형 스키마(외래 키, JOIN)를".
- Sentence connection → **separate sentences**: "~와 비슷하다 — 지역 변수가 ~" → "~와 비슷하다. 지역 변수가 ~".
- Term gloss → **prose**, or remove the dash and restructure: "**용어** — 설명이다" → "**용어**는 설명이다".
- Title/contents "제목 — 부제" → **move the subtitle into the first body sentence or use a single title**.
- **No colon substitution (settled policy)**: benelog’s original replaces dashes with colons (`제목: 부제`, `용어: 설명`), but **humanize rewrite outputs do not adopt that choice** (conversation replies under the `fluent-korean` output styles may use a colon; that is their own scope). Remove subtitle/gloss colons unless requested, consistently with C-10 (colon-subtitle headings) and C-3. Resolve dashes through **parentheses, sentence splitting, or prose**.
- Density target: At most 1–2 body dashes per document.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `[—–]`
- Keep: Preserve dashes inside **code blocks, inline code, configuration files, or quoted actual output** (e.g. `PASS — ...`) with code/quotation evidence. Also retain typographic range dashes (`2020–2024`).
### J-4. Excessive parenthetical explanation
- Repeated parenthetical explanations such as "(이는 ~을 의미한다)".
- Remedy: Integrate most parenthetical explanations into the body or delete them.
---
## L. Abstract metaphor lexicon — S2
Generic metaphors cover positions where concrete nouns/verbs belong, fitting grammatically into any context but forcing readers to ask for missing information. Original source: benelog’s "클로드 문체 없애기 지침". All rules are ANCHORED. Each rule’s **Keep** clause preserves legitimate physical/idiomatic usage. Given a candidate, the detector emits keep with an evidence token if its Keep condition applies; otherwise flag.
### L-1. Abstract overuse of "무너진다" [S2]
- Pattern: Metaphorical "무너진다" for failure/disintegration without actual collapse.
- Example: "이 가정이 **무너진다**" → "이 가정이 성립하지 않는다"
- Remedy: Specify what fails and how. In security contexts, use the actual event verb ("뚫린다").
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `무너진다|무너졌다|무너지는|무너질|무너져`
- Keep: Allow physical collapse and descriptions of its immediate consequences.
### L-2. Abstract overuse of "맞물린다" [S2]
- Pattern: Vaguely describing a relation as "맞물린다".
- Example: "두 정책이 **맞물려** 있다" → "두 정책이 서로를 전제한다 / A가 B의 조건이다"
- Remedy: Name the actual relation: causality, evidence, combination, use, or compatibility.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `맞물린다|맞물려|맞물리는|맞물린|맞물릴`
- Keep: Allow literal mechanical interlocking.
### L-3. Abstract overuse of "가른다/갈린다" [S2]
- Pattern: Metaphorical "가른다/갈린다" for distinction/decision.
- Example: "성능이 승패를 **가른다**" → "성능이 승패를 정한다 / 승패는 성능에 달렸다"
- Remedy: Be precise with "나뉜다", "구분한다", "정한다", "~에 달렸다".
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `가른다|가르는|가를|갈린다|갈리는|갈릴|갈렸다|갈라`
- Keep: Allow physical splitting, the idiom "의견이 갈린다", and branching metaphors established by context.
### L-4. Abstract overuse of "무겁다/가볍다" [S2]
- Pattern: Collapsing multiple dimensions (memory, latency, training volume, size) into a one-dimensional adjective.
- Example: "이 모델은 **무겁다**" → "이 모델은 추론 지연이 크다 / 메모리를 40GB 쓴다"
- Remedy: State the actual criterion: memory, size, time, or procedure.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `무겁다|무거운|무거워|가볍다|가벼운|가벼워`
- Keep: Allow physical weight and the idiom "말의 무게".
### L-5. Abstract overuse of "지점" [S2]
- Pattern: Replacing a part, passage, or place with "지점".
- Example: "논의가 갈리는 **지점**" → "논의가 갈리는 대목"
- Remedy: Use "부분", "곳", "대목", etc. Retain technical fixtures and actual places.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `지점`
- Keep: Allow actual places/technical uses such as coordinate or measurement points and bank branches.
### L-6. Abstract overuse of "축" [S2]
- Pattern: Metaphorical "축" for a criterion, cause, item, or component.
- Example: "비용이라는 **축**에서" → "비용이라는 기준에서"
- Remedy: Use "기준", "원인", "항목", "구성 요소".
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `(?:^|[\s(])축(?:을|이|은|의|으로|과|도|에서|에)`
- Keep: Allow actual 2D coordinate/graph axes. The detector keeps compounds such as 구축/축적.
### L-7. Abstract overuse of "겹" [S2]
- Pattern: Metaphorical "겹" for counts/relations.
- Example: "여러 **겹**의 의미" → "의미가 세 층으로 나뉜다 / 뜻이 두 가지다"
- Remedy: Give the actual count or concrete relation.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `여러 겹|겹겹|겹의|겹을|겹이|겹으로`
- Keep: Allow actual layers/physical overlap.
### L-8. Abstract overuse of "손잡이" [S2]
- Pattern: Metaphorical "손잡이" for control/settings.
- Example: "이 파라미터가 성능의 **손잡이**다" → "이 파라미터로 성능을 조절한다"
- Remedy: Specify what is adjusted and how.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `손잡이`
- Keep: Allow physical handles.
### L-9. Abstract overuse of "사실상" [S2]
- Pattern: Adding "사실상" as unnecessary cushioning/emphasis.
- Example: "**사실상** 표준이다" → retain if an established term; otherwise "표준이다".
- Remedy: Use only for established terms (de facto) or necessary precise qualification; otherwise delete/paraphrase.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `사실상`
- Keep: Allow established terms such as "사실상 표준(de facto)" and essential qualifications.
### L-10. Abstract overuse of "~인 셈이다" [S2]
- Pattern: Vaguely wrapping an ordinary conclusion in "~인 셈이다".
- Example: "비용을 절반으로 줄인 **셈이다**" → "비용을 절반으로 줄였다"
- Remedy: Use only to close a metaphor. State ordinary conclusions directly.
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `셈이다|셈입니다|셈이었다|셈이지`
- Keep: Allow conclusions closing an earlier metaphor/comparison.
### L-11. Abstract overuse of "결" [S2] · related extension absent from the gist
- Pattern: Covering character, direction, tone, or texture with the one-syllable metaphor "결".
- Example: "논의의 **결**이 다르다" → "논의의 방향이 다르다 / 성격이 다르다"
- Remedy: Specify "성격", "방향", "톤", or "질감".
- class: ANCHORED
- 표층형: `(?:^|\s)결(?:이|의|을|은|도|[\s.,])`
- Keep: Allow physical grain/texture such as 나뭇결, 물결, 살결. Compounds such as 결과, 결정, 연결 are excluded by the surface regex.
---
## Detection output schema (shared Detector → Rewriter contract) — v2.0
The detector takes prescan’s `02_candidates.json` and produces the following JSON. **Every ANCHORED candidate must be exhausted through either `findings` (flag) or `kept` (preserve), enforced in code by `reconcile.js`.**
```json
{
"meta": {
"input_length": 1820,
"detected_count": 37,
"ai_tell_density": 0.203,
"severity_weighted_score": 71.5,
"taxonomy_version": "2.0",
"taxonomy_hash": "<candidates.meta.taxonomy_hash를 그대로 echo>",
"coverage": {
"L-8": {"candidates": 3, "flagged": 2, "kept": 1},
"A-2": {"candidates": 6, "flagged": 6, "kept": 0}
}
},
"findings": [
{
"id": "f001",
"candidate_id": "A-2:142",
"category": "A-2",
"category_label": "번역투: ~를 통해 남발",
"severity": "S1",
"scope": "span",
"text_span": "데이터 분석을 통해",
"start": 142,
"end": 153,
"reason": "'통해'가 본문에서 6회 반복되어 경로 서술이 기계적",
"suggested_fix": "데이터를 분석해서"
}
],
"kept": [
{
"candidate_id": "L-8:512",
"rule_id": "L-8",
"reason": "물리적 문 손잡이를 가리킴 — 은유 아님",
"evidence": "문 손잡이"
}
],
"category_summary": {
"A": 12, "B": 3, "C": 2, "D": 8, "E": 1,
"F": 4, "G": 2, "H": 3, "I": 1, "J": 1, "L": 5
}
}
```
- `severity_weighted_score`: Weighted sum with S1=5, S2=2, S3=0.5, normalized to 0–100.
- `ai_tell_density`: Total detected span characters / total characters.
- **`candidate_id`**: `{rule_id}:{start}` assigned by prescan. The detector **echoes** candidate offsets instead of recalculating them.
- **`kept[]`**: ANCHORED candidates preserved without flagging. Verifiable `evidence` tokens are **required**; missing evidence makes reconcile FAIL (mass-keep prevention, H2).
- **`meta.coverage`**: Per-rule `{candidates, flagged, kept}` measures **decision completeness**. Detection recall (surface forms missed by the regex) is measured separately through `tests/gold/`; 100% coverage does not mean 100% recall.
- **COMPUTED rules**: A triggered rule requires a corresponding `findings` entry with scope=document. reconcile recalculates metrics to verify this.
### reconcile gate contract
`scripts/reconcile.js` enforces the following in the pipeline (violation exits 1 and stops execution):
1. Three-way taxonomy hash agreement: current file == candidates stamp == detection echo.
2. Exhaustion of every ANCHORED candidate (`candidates == flagged + kept`, zero unresolved).
3. A corresponding finding for every triggered COMPUTED rule.
## Version history
- **v1** (2026-04-24): Initial ten categories × 40+ subpatterns.
- **v1.1** (2026-04-24): Self-reaudit of the first practical case (rewriting an AI strategy column) promoted **seven patterns reproduced 2+ times**:
- `A-15` Abstract subject + generic verb (`X가 Y를 보여준다/제공한다`)
- `C-7` Three-part paragraph-opening formula "먼저·반면·결국"
- `C-8` Repeated symmetrical antithesis "A인가, B인가"
- `D-5` Personified abstract subjects ("두 지능의 충돌", "AI 대전")
- `D-6` Formulaic conclusion "~할 때입니다 / 시점입니다"
- `F-5` Abstract "~적 N" chains (에이전트적 자율성·기술적 토대)
- `H-4` Overuse of restating conjunction "즉"
- **v1.2** (2026-04-25): Promoted **three translationese/conjunction patterns** reproduced in public documents (procurement proposals and policy reports). Established the separate `public-procurement-style` skill (K–O categories).
- `A-20` `~에 대한` noun-phrase chains [S2]: literal English `of / for / about X`. Examples: "AI **에 대한** 이해**에 대한** 필요성**에 대한** 검토" / "보안**에 대한** 기준**에 대한** 수립". Remedy: direct object particles (`보안 기준을 수립`) or verb clauses (`보안을 검토`). Remove chains of two or more per sentence. *(Introduced as A-16 in v1.2; renumbered A-20 in v3.0 to resolve upstream ID conflicts.)*
- `A-21` Passive possibility `~될 수 있음` / `~될 수 있다` [S2]: combines A-8 (double passive) with A-10 (`~할 수 있다`), the most frequent residual pattern in public documents. Examples: "~가 **수행될 수 있음**", "~가 **확인될 수 있다**". Remedy: definite active statements (`~를 수행한다`, `~를 확인한다`). *(Introduced as A-17 in v1.2; renumbered A-21 in v3.0.)*
- `H-5` Consecutive paragraph openings `따라서·이에·이를 위해` [S2]: the same logical conjunction appears at the start of three or more consecutive paragraphs, a special case of H-1. Example: "따라서 ~한다. 이에 ~한다. 이를 위해 ~한다." Remove at least two; sentence content should reveal causality.
- **v2.0** (2026-07-13): Introduced the **rule-management model and runtime application guarantees**. (1) Added `class` (ANCHORED/COMPUTED/HOLISTIC) for all rules, with machine fields `표층형` for ANCHORED and `지표` for COMPUTED. (2) Added three deterministic scripts: `lint_taxonomy.js` (SSOT integrity), `prescan.js` (deterministic candidates), and `reconcile.js` (candidate exhaustion/hash agreement gates). (3) Added **L. Abstract metaphor lexicon** (L-1–L-11), based on benelog’s guidance: 무너진다·맞물린다·가른다·무겁다·지점·축·겹·손잡이·사실상·셈이다 plus the related extension 결. (4) Added `candidate_id`, `kept[]` (evidence tokens), and `meta.coverage` to the output schema. Backfilled surface/class fields in representative A–J rules (A-2·A-7·A-8·D-1·F-1·H-4·I-1·J-3·E-1); expand the rest incrementally, append-only.
- **v3.0** (2026-07-23): **Absorbed upstream im-not-ai v2.3.** (1) Thirteen new rules: A-16 (literal pronouns, translation contexts only), A-17 (hold), A-18 (left-branching adnominal modifiers, COMPUTED), A-19 (double particles, ANCHORED), C-9–C-12 (numeric indexing, colon headings, connective ending + comma at KatFish 4.84×, comma inclusion rate), D-7 (transformation formula), E-4–E-7 (simple-sentence dominance, clause length, POS diversity, honorifics), G-3 (safe-balance lexicon). (2) Five empirical recalibrations: A-2 S1→S2 (Choi 2016 rejection); C-1 S1→S2 with default preservation; C-8 S2→**S1** (negative antithesis AI 9.2×, G²=41.7, strongest measured signal) plus negative–positive variants; E-1 redefined as missing long sentences (11×); I-1 default preservation (humans twice as often). (3) Redefined B-2 to prohibit mechanical literal translation of technical terms. (4) Added the D-category prohibition on inserting target phrases during rewriting. (5) **Renumbered IDs**: local v1.2 A-16→A-20, A-17→A-21, adopting upstream IDs as canonical to resolve collisions. (6) Vendored `metrics_v1/v2` (KatFish measured baselines + Toral translationese T1–T8), `verify_gates.js` (four-axis convergence/non-destructive gates), and `tests/golden/checks.py` (MIT, epoko77-ai/im-not-ai); added seven COMPUTED metrics and ported `empirical-validation.md`.
- Extension rule: Add subpatterns only after at least two reproductions in practical input and near-absence among human writers. **ANCHORED promotion requires `표층형`; COMPUTED promotion requires `지표`** (enforced by lint).
- Related skill: See `public-procurement-style` for procurement-specific register deviations (K–O).
