## Mood exemptions and upstream exceptions
This section adds no rules. It records **which rules do not fire in which moods**. `references/register-axis.md` owns mood definitions and the discriminator.
### List/itemized-style exemption (E-2)
Upstream fluent-korean sentence-level rule 2 explicitly says it is not mandatory for headers and lists. That parenthetical sentence was lost during absorption, causing this skill to count nominal endings as defects in itemized documents. Restore the upstream safeguard.
| Target | E-2 application |
|---|---|
| Prose paragraph | Applies |
| Header row | Exempt |
| List item | Exempt |
| Itemized body (`□ ○ - ⇒`) | Exempt |
| Table cell | Exempt |
| Caption row | Exempt |
This exemption resolves most nominal-ending conflicts. The remaining case is dominance of nominal endings in prose paragraphs: the telegraphic style E-2 originally targets.
### Exceptions to the absent-human-voice rule (3.9)
Upstream noslop-ko rule 3.9 treats absence of a human voice as a defect but exempts only reports and technical documents, leaving half our main artifact types outside the exceptions. Procurement proposals default to a commitment register, where irregularity can cost points, yet 3.9 treats the absence of that irregularity as a defect. Port only after broadening the exceptions.
- Exceptions: reports, technical documents, **proposals**, **official documents**, **itemized writing**.
Do not apply 3.9 to proposals, official documents, or itemized writing.
Rule 3.9 is qualitative and cannot be caught mechanically, so false positives can silently lower a grade from A to B. Whenever 3.9 lowers a grade, log that fact and its evidence; otherwise a later audit cannot distinguish false positives from real defects.
### Placement of reverse checks
Promote the seven reverse-check items in noslop-ko part 4 to thresholds rather than exemptions. Their placement is in the grade-impact table in `references/register-invariants.md`.
